{"id": "Mercury_417147", "question": "The salt in ocean water comes from all of the following except", "step_idx": 0, "options": ["A): eroding land.", "B): melting glacial ice.", "C): volcanic emissions.", "D): reactions on the sea floor."], "correct": "B", "initial_cot": "(A) Eroding land contributes to the salt in ocean water through the process of weathering, where rocks are broken down into smaller particles that can be carried away by wind or water and eventually deposited into the ocean.\n(B) Melting glacial ice also contributes to the salt in ocean water. Glaciers are formed from snow that has been compressed over time, and as they melt, they release the salt and other minerals that were trapped within the ice.\n(C) Volcanic emissions can also contribute to the salt in ocean water. Volcanic eruptions release gases and minerals, including salt, into the atmosphere, which can eventually be deposited into the ocean.\n(D) Reactions on the sea floor are not a significant source of salt in ocean water. While chemical reactions can occur on the sea floor, they do not release significant amounts of salt into the ocean.", "initial_cot_probs": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0], "initial_probs": [0.15279598534107208, 0.3234691023826599, 0.19619391858577728, 0.3234691023826599], "prediction": 1, "cot_prediction": 3, "cot_step": "(A) Eroding land contributes to the salt in ocean water through the process of weathering, where rocks are broken down into smaller particles that can be carried away by wind or water and eventually deposited into the ocean.", "segmented_cot": ["(A) Eroding land contributes to the salt in ocean water through the process of weathering, where rocks are broken down into smaller particles that can be carried away by wind or water and eventually deposited into the ocean.", "(B) Melting glacial ice also contributes to the salt in ocean water.", "Glaciers are formed from snow that has been compressed over time, and as they melt, they release the salt and other miner