"The question asks for the part of an atom with the least mass. To answer this, we need to know the relative masses of the different parts of an atom.\nThe mass of an atom is primarily composed of its protons, neutrons, and electrons. The number of protons in an atom determines the element of an atom, and the number of neutrons can vary, leading to different isotopes of the same element. Electrons, on the other hand, have a negligible mass compared to protons and neutrons.\nThe mass of a proton is approximately 1 atomic mass unit (amu), and the mass of a neutron is also about 1 amu. The mass of an electron is much smaller, about 1/1836 that of a proton.\nTherefore, the part of an atom with the least mass is the electron.\nThe best answer is A.<|eot_id|>", "new_cot_probs": [1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0], "cot_prob": [-0.6488680243492126], "cot_step_prob": [-0.7235987186431885]}}}
